            <ArticleTitle>Ethical issues in palliative care.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>183-93, vii</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pop.2011.03.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ethical problems in medicine are common, especially when caring for patients at the end of life. However, many of these issues are not adequately identified in the outpatient setting. Primary care providers are in a unique and privileged position to identify ethical issues, prevent future conflicts, and help patients make medical decisions that are consistent with their individual values and preferences. This article describes some of the more common ethical issues faced by primary care physicians caring for patients with life-limiting illness.</AbstractText>
